/* book exam traps: Temperatures in T₂/T₁ = (v₁/v₂)γ−1 and in 1 − TL/TH must be in kelvin. Writing 1 − 27/627 instead of 1 − 300/900 gives 95.7% instead of 66.7%. | The air standard efficiency is not the efficiency of the real engine. It is an upper limit for that cycle shape; the real value is lower because of friction, heat loss, variable specific heats and incomplete burning. | The Carnot efficiency does not depend on the working fluid or on the volume ratio during heat addition. Only TH and TL appear. | In the isothermal steps heat equals work, q = RT ln(v₂/v₁). Students often write cvΔT, which is zero there. | A favourite question: "Why is the Carnot cycle not used in practice?" Answer with the low work ratio, the low mean effective pressure and the impossibility of fast isothermal heat transfer. */
