/* book exam traps: Cut-off ratio is v₃/v₂ in the Diesel cycle but v₄/v₃ in the Dual cycle, because the Dual cycle's constant-pressure part starts at state 3. Read the state numbers before using a formula. | "Cut-off at 6% of the stroke" means rc = 1 + 0.06(r − 1), not 1.06 and not 0.06r. | Use cp for heat added at constant pressure and cv for heat at constant volume. Using cv for the Diesel heat input makes qin 29% too small. | Keep temperatures in kelvin. A question that gives 27 °C at the start of compression means T₁ = 300 K; putting 27 into T₂ = T₁rγ−1 gives 62 K instead of 689 K. | The Otto formula 1 − 1/rγ−1 is not the Diesel efficiency. For the same r the Diesel value is lower; the Diesel engine wins in practice only because it can use a higher r. */
